# One doorway for the fast thinking, configured by settings

## Story

As a team shipping while it's still unclear which AI provider we'll rely on,
I want every request for a quick bit of AI text to go through one single place that's configured entirely by settings,
so that switching providers later is a settings change and never a code change — and a slow or broken provider never stalls a live call.

## What this is

This is the one and only doorway the rest of the system uses whenever it needs
a quick bit of AI text. Everything about which provider it talks to — which
one, where, and with what credentials — comes from settings, never from the
code itself. That way, when a provider gets rate-limited or we decide to swap
to another, it's just an edit to configuration, with nothing in the code to
touch.

Because it sits right on the live-call path, speed and safety come first. It
makes one quick attempt at the primary provider with a tight time limit. If
that fails and a backup provider is fully set up, it makes one more quick
attempt at the backup. The two attempts together stay inside a small, strict
time budget. If everything fails — bad configuration, a timeout, a garbled
response, anything — it simply comes back empty-handed rather than making the
caller wait or throwing an error at them.

## What "done" looks like

- One quick primary attempt on a tight timeout, and at most one backup
  attempt — and only when the backup is completely set up. A half-configured
  backup is treated as no backup at all.
- On any failure whatsoever, it returns nothing rather than raising. The caller
  never has to catch an error from it.
- It never leaks anything sensitive. The request text, the provider's address,
  and the credentials never appear in the log. Each call leaves only a short,
  safe note about how it went.
- For rehearsal and testing offline, there's a practice mode that returns a
  fixed canned response with no network access at all.
